// agent_task.ax — what an AI agent is ASKED to write.
//
// The task: "Score these candidate records for quality and flag the risky ones."
// A benign data-processing job. The agent writes the scorer below.
//
// The ONE thing the operator requires: the agent may do local compute only.
// It must NOT read arbitrary files, must NOT reach the network, must NOT spawn
// processes — because it's handling data the operator doesn't want exfiltrated.
//
// In Axon that requirement is not a code review, a policy doc, or a Docker flag.
// It's ONE LINE the compiler enforces before the program can run even once:
//
//     @[contained(fs: [], net: [], exec: none)]
//
//   fs:   []     no filesystem reads or writes
//   net:  []     no network — cannot call an LLM, cannot phone home
//   exec: none   no spawning curl/sh/anything
//
// Four safety layers protect this agent (R26/R27/R28 + @[contained]):
//   Layer 1 — @[contained]     compile-time capability proof (this annotation)
//   Layer 2 — runtime sandbox  @[contained] re-enforced at builtin dispatch
//   Layer 3 — R27 kill-switch  operator can halt the agent at any time (axon-os)
//   Layer 4 — R26 attestation  kernel image measured before the agent boots
//
// Run:    axon run   examples/flagship/agent_task.ax    -> exit 0, prints scores
// Check:  axon check examples/flagship/agent_task.ax    -> exit 0, compiles clean
// Demo:   examples/flagship/demo.sh                     -> all four layers
//
// This is the GOOD agent: it stays inside the sandbox, so it builds and runs.
// See agent_task_evil.ax for the same task written by an agent that tries to
// exfiltrate — and watch the COMPILER refuse to build it.

// The scoring function: purely local computation with a safety contract.
// @[verify(value >= 0)] is a runtime-enforced postcondition (Phase 5):
// the compiler proves it statically when possible; when not, exit 6 fires
// before an invalid score can reach the caller.
@[contained(fs: [], net: [], exec: none)]
@[verify(value >= 0)]
fn score(quality: i64, risk: i64, confidence: i64) -> i64 {
    let base = quality - risk * 2
    if confidence >= 80 { base } else { base / 2 }
}

@[test]
fn test_score() {
    assert_eq(score(90, 10, 90), 70)   // confident: 90 - 20
    assert_eq(score(90, 10, 50), 35)   // under-confident: halved
}

// @[goal] marks this as a goal-directed agent (R12 / Phase 7).
// The compiler emits I0001 info; the @[adaptive] optimizer can tune it.
// The axon-os run record (R21 section 3.4) captures every scored candidate
// in a hash-chained, tamper-evident audit log.
// TODO R28: when crates/axon-audit ships, link the run record to the
//           immutable capability ledger: axon-os audit verify --ledger <path>.
@[goal("maximize quality")]
fn main() -> i64 {
    // Three candidate records (quality, risk, confidence).
    let a = score(90, 10, 90)
    let b = score(60, 25, 70)
    let c = score(80, 5, 95)
    println("scores: a={to_str(a)} b={to_str(b)} c={to_str(c)}")
    println("best={to_str(c)}  (compiler-verified: no net/fs/exec; postcondition score>=0 enforced)")
    0
}
